### Rulings

- Ruling: use the manually created plan-scoped ledger because the prescribed shell helper is unavailable in this Windows sandbox — the directory and ignore behavior are identical; cost if wrong is only loss of helper-created metadata, which the ledger itself records.
- Ruling: treat `D5.BOUNDED_PAIR` as a catalog placeholder that cannot execute until T6 has two measured parent results — this preserves the design's controller-only composition and prevents speculative agent experiments; cost if wrong is delayed combined analysis, not fidelity leakage.
- Ruling (2026-08-26): preserve the Task 6 corrected replay as the default immutable authority and add Task 11 only as a separately selected, hash-bound `strict-proper-base` authority profile. Task 11 has no production I/L evidence (`institutional_fields=omitted_blank`, `allow_incomplete_fundamentals=true`, 82.22% C/A coverage), so it must remain `fidelity_incomplete` and must never be silently substituted for, or labelled as, full strict CANSLIM. Cost if wrong: one additional authority/profile path; the alternative corrupts reproduction identity and CANSLIM fidelity claims.
- Ruling (2026-08-26): do not metadata-relabel the completed Task 7 fact cache. Its 631,965 cached rows have null `net_income` and `roe`, predating the ROE materialization fix. Any reuse must be a new immutable, field-refreshing artifact with parent identity and fail-closed validation; cost if wrong: a selective refresh takes additional local processing, while relabeling would publish known-invalid A/ROE evidence.

Task 11A: strict proper-base authority profile dispatched after this ledger entry. It is additive only: preserve Task 6 defaults, select Task 11 explicitly, bind its exact artifact/row/summary identities, and keep the Task 11 profile incompatible with a full-I/L `--strict-canslim` claim. No new tests are to be added or run before the user-authorized build/diagnosis work is complete; direct artifact verification is allowed.

Task 11A scope correction (2026-08-26): the existing cached D0–D4 runner is not contract-faithful to Task 11 (it changes entry gates, market policy, fills, lifecycle, and does not replay the baseline). It must fail closed for `strict-proper-base-task11` rather than report a false reproduction. Expose Task 11 only through an explicit immutable artifact-verification route now; the separate engine-faithful artifact diagnostic will follow. Cost if wrong: delayed cached experiments versus a misleading CANSLIM/engine diagnosis.

Task 11A: implementation complete and task review ACCEPTED. `baseline.py` adds the closed Task 11 authority/profile and now binds the manifest digest before parsing; `pit_diagnosis.py` preserves Task 6 defaults, makes Task 11 verification-only, rejects Task 11 from cached `run`/`run-experiment`, rejects `--strict-canslim`, and provides `verify-baseline`. The existing checkpoint-rebind tool remains compatible. No tests were added or run per user direction. Deferred P2 for the later test phase: `tests/test_pit_diagnosis_baseline.py:148` constructs a synthetic authority without matching the manifest SHA and needs fixture maintenance for the new correct invariant.

Task 11B: artifact-faithful diagnosis dispatched after the user challenged sparse entries during the 2023–2025 bull market. It must read the sealed Task 11 ledgers only, after profile verification, and report the actual sequential funnel, next-open outcomes, holdings/cash, and exits. Cached D0–D4 remains deliberately excluded because it changes the production contract. No tests are to be added or run before the build/diagnosis phase is complete.

Task 11B: implementation complete and fresh final review ACCEPTED. New `task11_artifact_diagnosis.py` parses only digest-verified ephemeral byte snapshots of the canonical Task 11 manifest/summary/signal/daily/outcome/weekly/transaction ledgers; it rejects forged profiles (including subclasses), profile/manifest/artifact identity drift, and any funnel mismatch. `pit_diagnosis.py diagnose-task11-artifacts` is explicit and Task11-only. Main-task direct CLI verification reconciled 2023–25 as 375,703 evaluated -> 8,439 technical setups -> 173 qualified/attempted -> 131 executed and 42 next-open buy-zone rejections; it reported 2.822 average open holdings and 67.605% average cash. No tests were added or run, and no strategy/data/cache/optimizer/sealed-artifact changes occurred. The immediate order-loop/capacity hypothesis is disproven; the next diagnosis must audit the C/A/RS/composite inputs and exit lifecycle without weakening the entry contract.

Task 11C: read-only, engine-faithful C/A/RS/composite and lifecycle sub-audits dispatched in parallel. Their brief explicitly forbids edits, tests, cache/data/artifact mutations, optimizer interaction, and threshold changes; each conclusion must distinguish verified behavior from a possible PIT/contract gap or a later strategy choice.

Task 11C: both read-only sub-audits completed. Entry-contract audit independently reconstructed the production ledger with zero mismatches across 375,703 rows: most 2023–25 technical setup losses are finite C/A values below hard floors (5,712 C and 1,136 A), then RS/composite, not missing RS or a buy-loop rejection. PIT release/as-of behavior and reason logging matched the sealed engine; full C/A coverage remains 82.216% and I/L is omitted, so Task11 stays fidelity-incomplete. Lifecycle audit proved exposure is mathematically explained by 0.834 buys/week and mean 23.575-day natural full-exit life (predicted 2.810 versus actual 2.822 holdings); zero cash/capacity rejects. It discovered one material production contract gap: Task11 records `take_profit_pct=0.40` / `scale_out_fraction=0.50`, but normal runtime ignores both and always applies fixed global +10/+15/+20% 25%-each scale-out tiers. This requires an explicit user/contract decision before any engine edit because wiring those knobs changes strategy behavior; no thresholds or exits were changed by the audit.

Task 11D: C/A provenance drill-down design investigation dispatched. It is read-only and must determine whether the exact production evaluator can expose source, comparable period, public date, and unavailability reason for every technical C/A rejection from the sealed Task11 PIT bundle. It must not rebuild a portfolio, lower a threshold, or use future/provider data.

